window.AG_PRACTICE["pulse"] = {"id":"pulse","axis":"dx","seal":"診","scene":"art/scene-clinic.jpg","title":"Pulse Reading","titleZh":"脈象判讀","sub":"Depth, rate, width, force, shape — five questions, one finger","subZh":"位、數、形、勢——一指五問","mentor":{"key":"jing","nm":"Jìng Gū","nmZh":"靜姑","line":"\"Do not try to name the pulse first. Ask it five questions instead: how deep, how fast, how wide, how strong, what shape. The name arrives on its own.\"","lineZh":"「不要一上手就想給脈取名字。先問它五件事：多深、多快、多粗、多有力、什麼形狀。名字自己會來。」"},"card":{"h":"Five Questions, One Finger 一指五問","hZh":"","p":"Depth tells you exterior or interior. Rate tells you cold or heat. Force tells you deficiency or excess. Width tells you whether blood fills the vessel. Shape — slippery, choppy, wiry, tight — names the pathogen. Most pulses are two or three of these at once.","pZh":"位以辨表裡，數以辨寒熱，勢以辨虛實，形以辨血之盈虧；至於滑、澀、弦、緊，則指其邪。臨床之脈，多為二三象並見。"},"items":[{"k":"mc","q":"Light touch finds it easily; heavy pressure loses it. Which pulse?","qZh":"舉之有餘，按之不足，是何脈？","opts":[{"t":"Floating 浮","c":"浮脈","ok":1,"note":"Floating means the qi has come to the surface — usually an exterior pattern in a new illness. But in a long illness a floating pulse without force can mean yang floating outward from an empty root, which is serious. Read it with the story. 浮者，氣趨於表，新病多屬表證。然久病脈浮而無力，為虛陽外越，其候非輕。當合病程而讀。"},{"t":"Deep 沉","c":"沉脈","ok":0,"note":"Deep is the opposite: light touch finds nothing, you must press to the bone. It means the disease is interior. 沉者反是：輕取不應，重按始得，主裡證。"},{"t":"Slippery 滑","c":"滑脈","ok":0,"note":"Slippery describes the *shape* of the wave, not its depth — smooth and rolling like a bead. 滑言其形，非言其位，往來流利如珠走盤。"},{"t":"Rapid 數","c":"數脈","ok":0,"note":"Rapid is a rate: more than five beats to one breath. 數者言其至數，一息五至以上。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Fewer than four beats to one breath of the physician. Which pulse, and what does it usually mean?","qZh":"一息不足四至，何脈？主何？","opts":[{"t":"Slow 遲 — usually cold","c":"遲脈 — 多主寒","ok":1,"note":"Cold slows movement. Slow *with force* suggests cold excess congealing; slow *without force* suggests yang deficiency. The one trap: heat that has bound solidly in the interior can also slow the pulse, so read the rest of the case. 寒則遲。遲而有力為實寒凝滯，遲而無力為陽虛。惟邪熱結聚於裡者，脈亦可遲，故當合參。"},{"t":"Moderate 緩 — normal","c":"緩脈 — 平脈","ok":0,"note":"A moderate pulse is about four beats per breath and relaxed — that is close to normal, or it suggests damp. Fewer than four is slow. 緩脈約四至而和緩，近於平脈，或主濕。不足四至者為遲。"},{"t":"Weak 弱 — deficiency","c":"弱脈 — 主虛","ok":0,"note":"Weak describes force, not rate: deep, fine and without strength. 弱言其勢，不言其數：沉細而無力。"},{"t":"Knotted 結 — qi constraint","c":"結脈 — 主氣結","ok":0,"note":"Knotted is slow *with irregular pauses*. The pauses are what define it. 結脈遲而時有一止，止無定數。其要在「止」。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Rolling and smooth under the finger, like a bead running on a plate. Which pulse?","qZh":"往來流利，如珠走盤，是何脈？","opts":[{"t":"Slippery 滑","c":"滑脈","ok":1,"note":"Slippery means something substantial is moving in the vessel: phlegm, food retention, or damp-heat. It is also normal in pregnancy and can be normal in a healthy young person, so never read it alone. 滑者，脈中有形之物往來：痰飲、食積、實熱。孕婦及形體壯實之少年亦可見之，故不可獨憑一脈。"},{"t":"Choppy 澀","c":"澀脈","ok":0,"note":"Choppy is the opposite — rough and hesitant, like a light knife scraping bamboo. It means blood or essence is short, or blood is stagnant. 澀者反是，往來艱澀，如輕刀刮竹。主精血虧少或血瘀氣滯。"},{"t":"Wiry 弦","c":"弦脈","ok":0,"note":"Wiry is straight and taut along its length, like pressing a lute string. 弦者端直以長，如按琴弦。"},{"t":"Tight 緊","c":"緊脈","ok":0,"note":"Tight is taut and bounding, like a twisted rope — it flicks at the finger. Cold and pain. 緊者繃急彈指，如牽繩轉索。主寒、主痛、主宿食。"}]},{"k":"match","q":"Match each pulse to what it most often indicates.","qZh":"脈象配主病。","pairs":[{"l":"Wiry 弦","lZh":"","r":"Liver / Gallbladder, pain, phlegm-fluid","rZh":"肝膽病、痛證、痰飲"},{"l":"Tight 緊","lZh":"","r":"Cold, pain, food retention","rZh":"寒、痛、宿食"},{"l":"Choppy 澀","lZh":"","r":"Blood stasis, essence and blood depletion","rZh":"血瘀、精血虧少"},{"l":"Surging 洪","lZh":"","r":"Blazing heat in the qi level","rZh":"氣分熱盛"},{"l":"Thready 細","lZh":"","r":"Qi and blood deficiency, damp","rZh":"氣血兩虛、諸虛勞損、主濕"}],"note":"Notice that wiry and tight both feel taut. Wiry is straight and steady along its length; tight is springy and rolls under the finger like a rope being twisted. Wiry sends you to the Liver, tight sends you to cold. 弦緊皆有繃急之感。弦者端直而勢定，緊者彈指如轉索。弦當責肝，緊當責寒。"},{"k":"mc","q":"Which pulse most typically accompanies blood deficiency?","qZh":"血虛之脈，何者最典型？","opts":[{"t":"Thready 細 — thin as a thread but clearly felt","c":"細脈 — 如線而應指分明","ok":1,"note":"Blood is what fills the vessel, so when blood is short the vessel is narrow. Thready plus weak adds qi deficiency; thready plus rapid adds yin deficiency with empty heat. The width is the blood; what accompanies it names the rest. 血充脈道，血虛則脈道不充而細。細而弱者兼氣虛，細而數者兼陰虛內熱。細言其血，兼象言其餘。"},{"t":"Surging 洪","c":"洪脈","ok":0,"note":"Surging is broad and forceful, coming like a wave — that is heat with substance, the opposite of emptiness. 洪脈極大有力，來盛去衰，主實熱，與虛適反。"},{"t":"Slippery 滑","c":"滑脈","ok":0,"note":"Slippery means the vessel is over-full of something. A blood-deficient vessel is under-filled. 滑者脈中有物而盈，血虛者脈道不充。"},{"t":"Tight 緊","c":"緊脈","ok":0,"note":"Tight is cold and pain, and it has force. 緊主寒痛，且其勢有力。"}]},{"k":"match","q":"Match each position to the organ it reflects on the LEFT wrist.","qZh":"左手三部配臟。","pairs":[{"l":"Cun 寸 (index finger)","lZh":"","r":"Heart","rZh":"心"},{"l":"Guan 關 (middle finger)","lZh":"","r":"Liver and Gallbladder","rZh":"肝膽"},{"l":"Chi 尺 (ring finger)","lZh":"","r":"Kidney","rZh":"腎"}],"note":"On the right wrist: cun is Lung, guan is Spleen and Stomach, chi is Kidney (the gate of vitality, 命門). A weak chi on both sides in an older patient is one of the readings that sends you to the Kidney. 右手：寸候肺，關候脾胃，尺候腎（命門）。年高而兩尺俱弱者，當察腎。"},{"k":"mc","q":"A patient has a deep, slow pulse WITH force. Excess or deficiency?","qZh":"脈沉遲而有力，屬虛屬實？","opts":[{"t":"Excess — cold congealing in the interior","c":"實 — 裡實寒凝","ok":1,"note":"Force is the deciding word. Deep and slow with force is a real pathogen: interior cold congealing, treated by warming and dispersing. Deep and slow *without* force is yang deficiency, treated by warming and tonifying. One syllable, two opposite treatments. 有力二字定其虛實。沉遲有力為實寒內結，治以溫散；沉遲無力為陽虛，治以溫補。一字之差，二治相反。"},{"t":"Deficiency — yang deficiency","c":"虛 — 陽虛","ok":0,"note":"That is deep and slow *without* force. Adding force changes the answer. 彼為沉遲而無力。有力則不然。"},{"t":"Cannot be determined from the pulse","c":"據脈不可辨","ok":0,"note":"Force is precisely how the pulse answers the excess-deficiency question. It can be determined, and then confirmed against the rest of the case. 脈之有力無力，正所以答虛實之問。可辨，而後以他症相參。"},{"t":"Exterior cold","c":"表寒","ok":0,"note":"Exterior patterns float. A deep pulse says the illness is inside. 表證脈浮。沉則病在裡。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the correct sequence for taking a pulse?","qZh":"診脈之常法為何？","opts":[{"t":"Locate guan first with the middle finger, then place index and ring finger","c":"先以中指定關，再下食指與無名指","ok":1,"note":"Middle finger on the styloid process to find guan 關, then the index falls on cun 寸 and the ring finger on chi 尺. Spacing follows the patient's height. Then feel at three depths — light, medium, deep — and compare left with right. 中指按高骨定關，食指自然落於寸，無名指落於尺。布指疏密隨病人身長。次分浮中沉三候，並比較左右。"},{"t":"Press hard first to find the deep level","c":"先重按以取沉候","ok":0,"note":"Pressing hard first flattens the vessel and you lose the floating level entirely — which is exactly the level an exterior pattern lives at. 一上手即重按，則脈道受壓，浮候盡失，而表證正在浮候。"},{"t":"Use one finger and move it along","c":"一指遊走三部","ok":0,"note":"Three positions must be compared with each other, which needs three fingers resting at once. 三部貴在互較，故三指並下。"},{"t":"Take it immediately after the patient climbs the stairs","c":"病人登樓即診","ok":0,"note":"Exertion, emotion, food and cold all move the pulse. Let the patient settle for a short while, ideally with the arm level with the heart. 勞、情、食、寒皆能動脈。當令病人稍安，臂與心平。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Which pulse pair is easiest to confuse, and what separates them?","qZh":"何二脈最易混淆？其別安在？","opts":[{"t":"Thready 細 and Weak 弱 — weak is also deep and forceless","c":"細與弱 — 弱者更兼沉而無力","ok":1,"note":"Thready describes width alone; weak is deep, thready AND without force. So every weak pulse is thready, but not every thready pulse is weak. Naming the extra qualities is how you get from a pulse to a pattern. 細但言其形；弱者沉、細而無力，三者俱備。故弱必細，細未必弱。多說一象，便近一層病機。"},{"t":"Floating 浮 and Rapid 數 — they mean the same thing","c":"浮與數 — 二者義同","ok":0,"note":"They are different axes entirely: floating is depth, rapid is rate. They frequently occur together in wind-heat, but they are not the same reading. 二者異軸：浮言位，數言至數。風熱多並見，然非一事。"},{"t":"Deep 沉 and Slow 遲 — both mean cold","c":"沉與遲 — 皆主寒","ok":0,"note":"Deep means interior; slow means cold. Interior heat also gives a deep pulse. 沉主裡，遲主寒。裡熱亦可脈沉。"},{"t":"Wiry 弦 and Surging 洪 — both are strong","c":"弦與洪 — 皆為有力","ok":0,"note":"Both have force but the shapes are unmistakable: wiry is narrow and taut, surging is broad and wave-like. 二者雖俱有力，而形迥異：弦細而繃，洪大而如波。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"The pulse says heat but every other sign says cold. What should you do?","qZh":"脈主熱而諸症皆寒，當如何？","opts":[{"t":"Weigh which is true and which is false, and say so explicitly","c":"辨其從舍，明言取捨","ok":1,"note":"This is 舍脈從症 or 舍症從脈 — abandoning one in favour of the other. The rule is to follow whichever reflects the root: in a long illness with a sudden odd pulse, trust the symptoms; in an acute illness where symptoms are ambiguous, trust the pulse. Either way, state the reasoning rather than averaging the two. 此即捨脈從症、捨症從脈。取其能反映病本者：久病而脈忽異，多從症；新病而症模糊，多從脈。要在明其所以取捨，不可含混調和。"},{"t":"Always trust the pulse","c":"一概從脈","ok":0,"note":"The pulse can be misled by a recent meal, exertion, emotion, or an unusual anatomy. It is one of four examinations, not a verdict. 脈可為食、勞、情志、體異所惑。四診之一耳，非判詞。"},{"t":"Always trust the symptoms","c":"一概從症","ok":0,"note":"Symptoms can also mislead — true cold with false heat is exactly the case where the pulse saves you. 症亦可惑：真寒假熱者，正賴脈以決之。"},{"t":"Take the average of the two","c":"折中而取","ok":0,"note":"Averaging a contradiction gives a treatment that fits neither. Decide, and record why. 矛盾而折中，則所治二者皆不合。當決之，且記其故。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Which finding would make you slow down and consider referral rather than reach for needles?","qZh":"何種脈症當令你緩下，轉而考慮轉診？","opts":[{"t":"A pulse that stops at regular intervals in a breathless, swollen patient","c":"脈止有定數，兼氣促浮腫","ok":1,"note":"A regularly intermittent pulse 代脈 in a patient who is breathless and swelling can reflect serious organ weakness. Classical texts already treat it as grave. In modern practice this belongs with a physician who can investigate it properly — a game like this one is for learning patterns, not for making that call. 代脈而兼喘促浮腫者，古籍已視為臟氣衰微之候。今日臨證，此當交由能查者診之。本遊戲所習者辨證之理，非代醫者之判。"},{"t":"A slightly rapid pulse in a nervous first-time patient","c":"初診而略數","ok":0,"note":"Worth noting and worth re-taking once the patient settles. Not alarming on its own. 可記，且宜稍安再診。單此一象，不足為慮。"},{"t":"A slippery pulse in a woman of childbearing age","c":"育齡女子見滑脈","ok":0,"note":"Ask about the menstrual cycle before anything else — this is a normal finding in pregnancy. It changes what you do, but it is not a red flag. 當先問月經。孕婦見滑為常，此改所治，非為險兆。"},{"t":"A wiry pulse in someone who is irritable","c":"性急而脈弦","ok":0,"note":"Entirely consistent with a Liver pattern. Ordinary clinic. 與肝證相合，臨床常見。"}]}]};
